# Security Evaluation

## Findings

### SEC-001: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) via Unvalidated Proxy URL

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | SEC-001 |
| **Severity** | **Critical** |
| **CWE** | CWE-918 (Server-Side Request Forgery) |
| **Location** | Lines 4–7 |

**Description:**

The `targetUrl` parameter on line 4 is taken directly from user input (`req.query.url`) and passed without any validation or allowlist check to `fetch()` on line 5. This is a textbook SSRF vulnerability. An attacker can supply arbitrary URLs including:

- **Internal network resources:** `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` (AWS metadata), `http://localhost:6379/` (Redis), `http://10.0.0.1/admin`
- **Cloud provider metadata endpoints** to steal IAM credentials
- **`file://` protocol** (depending on the `fetch` implementation) to read local files

The response body is then returned verbatim to the attacker on line 7 (`res.send(body)`), making this a **full-read SSRF** — the attacker sees the entire response content.

**Evidence:**

```typescript
// Line 4: unsanitized user input
const targetUrl = req.query.url as string;
// Line 5: directly used in outbound request
const response = await fetch(targetUrl);
// Line 7: full response leaked to caller
res.send(body);
```

**Remediation:**

Implement URL validation with a strict allowlist of permitted domains/schemes, and block requests to private/reserved IP ranges:

```typescript
import express from "express";
import { URL } from "url";
import ipaddr from "ipaddr.js";
import dns from "dns/promises";

const ALLOWED_SCHEMES = new Set(["https:"]);
const ALLOWED_DOMAINS = new Set(["api.example.com", "cdn.example.com"]);

async function isPrivateIp(hostname: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const addresses = await dns.resolve4(hostname);
  return addresses.some((addr) => {
    const parsed = ipaddr.parse(addr);
    const range = parsed.range();
    return range !== "unicast"; // blocks private, loopback, linkLocal, etc.
  });
}

app.get("/proxy", async (req, res) => {
  const targetUrl = req.query.url;
  if (typeof targetUrl !== "string") {
    return res.status(400).send("Missing url parameter");
  }

  let parsed: URL;
  try {
    parsed = new URL(targetUrl);
  } catch {
    return res.status(400).send("Invalid URL");
  }

  if (!ALLOWED_SCHEMES.has(parsed.protocol)) {
    return res.status(400).send("Scheme not allowed");
  }

  if (!ALLOWED_DOMAINS.has(parsed.hostname)) {
    return res.status(400).send("Domain not allowed");
  }

  if (await isPrivateIp(parsed.hostname)) {
    return res.status(400).send("Internal targets not allowed");
  }

  const response = await fetch(targetUrl);
  const body = await response.text();
  res.send(body);
});
```

---

### SEC-002: Open Redirect / Response Proxy Without Content-Type Sanitization

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | SEC-002 |
| **Severity** | **Medium** |
| **CWE** | CWE-116 (Improper Encoding or Escaping of Output) |
| **Location** | Lines 6–7 |

**Description:**

The response body from an arbitrary external URL is forwarded to the client on line 7 via `res.send(body)` without setting or sanitizing the `Content-Type` header. Express's `res.send()` with a string argument defaults to `text/html`. If the proxied resource contains HTML with embedded JavaScript, it will be rendered as active HTML in the user's browser under the application's origin — effectively turning this into a stored/reflected XSS vector controlled entirely by the attacker (who chooses which URL to proxy).

**Evidence:**

```typescript
// Line 6-7: raw external content returned as text/html (Express default)
const body = await response.text();
res.send(body);
```

**Remediation:**

Force a safe content type and add security headers:

```typescript
res.set("Content-Type", "text/plain; charset=utf-8");
res.set("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff");
res.send(body);
```

Or better, use `helmet` middleware on the app to apply security headers globally.

---

## Summary

| Finding | Severity | Confidence |
|---------|----------|------------|
| SEC-001: SSRF via unvalidated proxy URL | Critical | 99% |
| SEC-002: Proxied content served as HTML | Medium | 95% |

## Overall Score: **12 / 100**

## Verdict: **FAIL**

This endpoint is an open SSRF proxy that allows any user to make the server fetch arbitrary URLs — including internal network resources and cloud metadata endpoints — and return the full response. This is one of the highest-impact web vulnerabilities (OWASP Top 10 A10:2021). The code requires fundamental redesign with URL allowlisting, scheme restriction, and private IP blocking before it can be considered safe.

# Judge Authentication — Evaluation Report

## Findings

### AUTH-001: JWT Signature Not Verified — `jwt.decode()` Used Instead of `jwt.verify()`

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | AUTH-001 |
| **Severity** | **CRITICAL** |
| **Confidence** | 99% |
| **Location** | Line 5: `const decoded = jwt.decode(token);` |

**Description:**

The middleware uses `jwt.decode(token)` which **only base64-decodes the token payload without verifying the cryptographic signature**. This means any attacker can forge an arbitrary JWT with `{"role": "admin"}` in the payload, and the middleware will accept it as authentic.

Per the [jsonwebtoken documentation](https://github.com/auth0/node-jsonwebtoken#jwtdecodetoken--options):

> *"This will NOT verify whether the signature is valid. You should NOT use this for untrusted messages. You most likely want to use `jwt.verify` instead."*

**Concrete exploit:**

```bash
# Attacker crafts a token with no secret at all:
echo -n '{"alg":"none","typ":"JWT"}' | base64url  # header
echo -n '{"role":"admin","sub":"attacker"}' | base64url  # payload
# Concatenate as header.payload. — accepted by jwt.decode()
```

The role check on line 6 (`(decoded as any).role === "admin"`) provides zero security because the attacker controls every field in the decoded payload.

**Recommendation:**

Replace `jwt.decode()` with `jwt.verify()`, specifying an explicit secret/key and restricting the allowed algorithms:

```typescript
import jwt from "jsonwebtoken";

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(" ")[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).send("No token");
  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET!, {
      algorithms: ["HS256"],
      // issuer: "your-app",
      // audience: "your-api",
    });
    if ((decoded as any).role === "admin") {
      req.user = decoded;
      next();
    } else {
      res.status(403).send("Forbidden");
    }
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(401).send("Invalid token");
  }
});
```

**References:**
- OWASP: [JSON Web Token Cheat Sheet — Always Verify Signatures](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/JSON_Web_Token_for_Java_Cheat_Sheet.html)
- NIST 800-63b §7.1: Verifier requirements for token-based authentication
- CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity

---

## Summary

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Total Findings | 1 |
| Critical | 1 |
| High | 0 |
| Medium | 0 |
| Low | 0 |
| Info | 0 |

## Overall Score: **5 / 100**

## Verdict: **FAIL**

**Rationale:** This middleware performs **no authentication whatsoever**. Using `jwt.decode()` instead of `jwt.verify()` means the token signature is never checked, rendering the entire auth gate useless. Any client can craft a token granting themselves admin access. This is a textbook critical authentication bypass — the single most consequential vulnerability an auth middleware can have.
