# Security Evaluation Report

## Findings

### SEC-001: JWT Token Decoded Without Signature Verification

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Severity** | **CRITICAL** |
| **CWE** | CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Authenticity |
| **Confidence** | 99% |
| **Location** | Line 5: `jwt.decode(token)` |

**Description:**

The code uses `jwt.decode(token)` instead of `jwt.verify(token, secret)`. The `jwt.decode()` function performs **no signature verification whatsoever** — it simply base64-decodes the payload. This means any attacker can craft an arbitrary JWT with `{"role": "admin"}` in the payload, and the middleware on line 6 will grant admin access.

This is not a theoretical risk. The entire authorization gate (lines 6–8) depends on the `role` claim from the decoded token, but nothing validates that the token was actually issued by the server.

**Exploit:**

```bash
# Attacker crafts a token with no signature — jwt.decode() will happily parse it
echo -n '{"alg":"none","typ":"JWT"}' | base64url  # header
echo -n '{"role":"admin","sub":"attacker"}' | base64url  # payload
# Send: Authorization: Bearer <header>.<payload>.
```

**Remediation:**

Replace `jwt.decode()` with `jwt.verify()`, specifying the signing secret/key and explicitly restricting the allowed algorithms:

```typescript
import jwt from "jsonwebtoken";

const JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET!;

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(" ")[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).send("No token");

  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET, {
      algorithms: ["HS256"], // Explicit algorithm restriction
    });

    if (decoded && (decoded as any).role === "admin") {
      req.user = decoded;
      next();
    } else {
      res.status(403).send("Forbidden");
    }
  } catch (err) {
