# Performance Engineering Review

## Findings

### PERF-001: N+1 Query Pattern
**Severity:** High
**Rule:** Database Queries (N+1)
**Location:** Lines 3–6

**Evidence:** Line 3 executes one query to fetch all orders. Then the `for` loop on lines 4–6 executes one additional query *per order* (`SELECT * FROM products WHERE order_id = $1`). For a user with N orders, this results in N+1 total database queries, each incurring a network round-trip to the database.

**Impact:** If a user has 100 orders, this issues 101 sequential database queries. Each round-trip might cost 1–5ms on a local network, meaning 100–500ms of pure latency spent waiting on the network—latency that scales linearly with the number of orders.

**Recommendation:** Replace with a single JOIN query or a batched `IN` query:

```typescript
// Option A: JOIN (single query)
async function getOrdersWithProducts(userId: string) {
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT o.*, p.id AS product_id, p.name AS product_name, p.price
     FROM orders o
     LEFT JOIN products p ON p.order_id = o.id
     WHERE o.user_id = $1`,
    [userId]
  );
  // Group rows by order
  const ordersMap = new Map();
  for (const row of rows) {
    if (!ordersMap.has(row.id)) {
      ordersMap.set(row.id, { ...row, products: [] });
    }
    if (row.product_id) {
      ordersMap.get(row.id).products.push({
        id: row.product_id,
        name: row.product_name,
        price: row.price,
      });
    }
  }
  return [...ordersMap.values()];
}

// Option B: Batched IN query (2 queries total)
async function getOrdersWithProducts(userId: string) {
  const orders = await db.query(
    "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = $1",
    [userId]
  );
  if (orders.length === 0) return [];

  const orderIds = orders.map((o) => o.id);
  const products = await db.query(
    "SELECT * FROM products WHERE order_id = ANY($1)",
    [orderIds]
  );

  const productsByOrder = new Map();
  for (const p of products) {
    if (!productsByOrder.has(p.order_id)) {
      productsByOrder.set(p.order_id, []);
    }
    productsByOrder.get(p.order_id).push(p);
  }

  return orders.map((order) => ({
    ...order,
    products: productsByOrder.get(order.id) ?? [],
  }));
}
```

Both options reduce the query count from N+1 to 1 or 2, eliminating the linear round-trip scaling.

