### PERF-001: Synchronous bcrypt blocks the event loop
**Severity:** Critical
**Location:** Lines 12-13, 29

**Evidence:** `bcrypt.genSaltSync(12)`, `bcrypt.hashSync(password, salt)`, and `bcrypt.compareSync(password, user.password)` are all synchronous calls in Express route handlers. bcrypt with a cost factor of 12 takes ~250-400ms per hash on typical hardware. During this time, the Node.js event loop is completely blocked — no other requests can be served by this process.

**Impact:** Under even modest concurrency (e.g., 10 simultaneous registrations), requests will queue sequentially, producing tail latencies of several seconds. A login endpoint with `compareSync` (line 29) has the same problem, meaning every authentication attempt blocks all traffic.

**Recommendation:**

```javascript
// Before (blocks event loop for ~300ms per call)
const salt = bcrypt.genSaltSync(12);
const hash = bcrypt.hashSync(password, salt);

// After (non-blocking, uses libuv thread pool)
app.post('/register', async (req, res) => {
  const { email, password } = req.body;
  const salt = await bcrypt.genSalt(12);
  const hash = await bcrypt.hash(password, salt);
  // ...
});

app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  // ...
  if (user && await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password)) {
    // ...
  }
});
```

