### PERF-002: Synchronous file I/O in request handlers
**Severity:** Critical
**Location:** Lines 16-18 (`/register`), Line 26 (`/login`)

**Evidence:** `fs.readFileSync('./users.json', 'utf8')` and `fs.writeFileSync('./users.json', ...)` are called inside route handlers. These block the event loop for the duration of the disk I/O. Combined with the bcrypt blocking (PERF-001), each `/register` request blocks the process for the full duration of hashing + two file operations.

**Impact:** The `/login` endpoint reads the entire file synchronously on every request. As `users.json` grows, this becomes increasingly expensive. At 10,000 users (~1MB JSON), every login parses and deserializes the full file while blocking all other connections.

**Recommendation:**

```javascript
const fs = require('fs').promises;

app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const { email, password } = req.body;
  const data = await fs.readFile('./users.json', 'utf8');
  const users = JSON.parse(data);
  // ...
});
```

For production use, a database with indexed lookups would eliminate the full-file read entirely.

