### CYBER-001: LDAP Injection via Unsanitized User Input

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **CRITICAL** |
| **CWE** | CWE-90 (LDAP Injection) |
| **OWASP** | A03:2021 – Injection |
| **Line** | 6 |
| **Confidence** | 99% |

**Description:**

On **line 6**, the `username` and `password` parameters are concatenated directly into an LDAP search filter string without any sanitization or escaping:

```java
String filter = "(&(uid=" + username + ")(userPassword=" + password + "))";
```

This is a textbook LDAP injection vulnerability. An attacker can manipulate the filter logic to bypass authentication entirely.

**Exploit Scenario:**

An attacker supplies:
- `username`: `*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*`
- `password`: anything

This transforms the filter into:

```
(&(uid=*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))
```

The first filter `(&(uid=*)(uid=*))` matches **every user** in the directory. Since `results.hasMore()` on line 10 only checks if at least one result exists, the attacker is authenticated without knowing any valid credentials.

An even simpler bypass — `username = *` — would match all `uid` entries.

**Remediation:**

Escape all LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`, `/`) in user-supplied values before embedding them in filters. Use a dedicated escaping method:

```java
import javax.naming.ldap.Rfc2253Parser; // or a manual approach

public boolean authenticate(String username, String password) {
    try {
        // Escape LDAP special characters per RFC 4515
        String safeUsername = escapeLdapFilter(username);
        String safePassword = escapeLdapFilter(password);
        String filter = "(&(uid=" + safeUsername + ")(userPassword=" + safePassword + "))";
        // ...
    } catch (Exception e) {
        return false;
    }
}

private static String escapeLdapFilter(String input) {
    if (input == null) return "";
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
        switch (c) {
            case '\\': sb.append("\\5c"); break;
            case '*':  sb.append("\\2a"); break;
            case '(':  sb.append("\\28"); break;
            case ')':  sb.append("\\29"); break;
            case '\0': sb.append("\\00"); break;
            default:   sb.append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

Alternatively, use the **OWASP ESAPI** library's `Encoder.encodeForLDAP()` or Spring LDAP's `LdapEncoder.filterEncode()`.

