**Description**

The `url` query parameter (line 6) is taken directly from `req.query.url` and passed without any validation, allowlisting, or DNS resolution check to `fetch()` on line 9. An attacker can supply arbitrary URLs, forcing the server to make requests to:

- **Cloud metadata services**: `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` (AWS), `http://metadata.google.internal/` (GCP), `http://169.254.169.254/metadata/instance` (Azure) — leaking IAM credentials, tokens, and instance identity.
- **Internal network services**: `http://internal-api:8080/admin`, `http://localhost:6379/` (Redis), `http://127.0.0.1:3306/` — port-scanning and accessing unauthenticated internal APIs.
- **Arbitrary file read** (depending on the `fetch` implementation): `file:///etc/passwd`.

The response body is then returned verbatim to the attacker on line 11 (`res.send(data)`), making this a **full-read SSRF** — the attacker sees the entire response content.

**Exploit Example**

```
GET /fetch?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ HTTP/1.1
```

This returns AWS IAM role names; a follow-up request retrieves temporary credentials (`AccessKeyId`, `SecretAccessKey`, `Token`), enabling full AWS account compromise.

**Remediation**

1. **Allowlist permitted domains/schemes** and reject everything else.
2. **Resolve the hostname before making the request** and block requests to private/reserved IP ranges (RFC 1918, link-local 169.254.x.x, loopback, etc.).
3. **Restrict the URL scheme** to `https://` only.
4. Use a dedicated SSRF-protection library such as [`ssrf-req-filter`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ssrf-req-filter) or implement a custom `dns.lookup` guard.

```typescript
import { URL } from "url";
import dns from "dns/promises";
import { isPrivate } from "ip"; // npm package "ip"

const ALLOWED_SCHEMES = new Set(["https:"]);

app.get("/fetch", async (req, res) => {
  const rawUrl = req.query.url as string;
  if (!rawUrl) return res.status(400).send("Missing url parameter");

  let parsed: URL;
  try {
    parsed = new URL(rawUrl);
  } catch {
    return res.status(400).send("Invalid URL");
  }

  // 1. Scheme allowlist
  if (!ALLOWED_SCHEMES.has(parsed.protocol)) {
    return res.status(400).send("Disallowed scheme");
  }

  // 2. Resolve hostname and block private IPs
  const { address } = await dns.lookup(parsed.hostname);
  if (isPrivate(address) || address === "127.0.0.1" || address === "::1") {
    return res.status(400).send("Disallowed target");
  }

  // 3. Make the request (consider adding a timeout)
  const response = await fetch(rawUrl, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });
  const data = await response.text();
  res.send(data);
});
```

> **Note:** DNS rebinding can bypass the resolve-then-fetch pattern. For production use, pin the resolved IP into the request via a custom `fetch` agent, or use a library that handles this atomically.

