| **CWE** | CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data |

**Evidence:**

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

A raw `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` call is made directly on data received from an unauthenticated TCP socket (line 9: `new ServerSocket(9999)`). There is **no filtering, no allowlist, and no `ObjectInputFilter`** applied before deserialization.

**Exploit Path:**

An attacker connects to port 9999 and sends a malicious serialized Java object. Using well-known gadget chains (e.g., from Apache Commons Collections, Spring, or other libraries on the classpath), the attacker achieves **Remote Code Execution (RCE)** on the server. This is one of the most reliably exploitable vulnerability classes in Java — tools like [ysoserial](https://github.com/frohoff/ysoserial) automate payload generation.

The `Command.execute()` call on line 30 makes this even more direct: an attacker crafts a class implementing `Command` whose `execute()` method runs arbitrary OS commands, and the server will invoke it without any validation.

**Remediation:**

1. **Preferred — Do not use Java native serialization for untrusted input.** Replace with a safe data format (JSON, Protocol Buffers, etc.):

```java
// Example using Jackson for JSON
ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
MyDto dto = mapper.readValue(socket.getInputStream(), MyDto.class);
```

2. **If native serialization is unavoidable,** apply a strict allowlist `ObjectInputFilter` (Java 9+):

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
ois.setObjectInputFilter(filterInfo -> {
    Class<?> clazz = filterInfo.serialClass();
    if (clazz == null) return ObjectInputFilter.Status.UNDECIDED;
    if (clazz == MyAllowedCommand.class) return ObjectInputFilter.Status.ALLOWED;
    return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
});
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

3. **Never** blindly invoke methods (like `execute()`) on deserialized objects from untrusted sources.

