### CYBER-002: Arbitrary Code Execution via Deserialized `Command` Interface (CWE-502 / CWE-94)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Severity** | 🔴 **CRITICAL** |
| **Confidence** | 97% |
| **Location** | Lines 28–30 |
| **CWE** | CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code |

**Evidence:**

```java
if (obj instanceof Command) {
    ((Command) obj).execute();
}
```

Any class implementing `Command extends Serializable` can be sent over the wire, deserialized, and have its `execute()` method invoked. The `Command` interface (line 34) has no restrictions on implementations. An attacker provides their own `Command` implementation:

```java
public class MaliciousCommand implements Command {
    public void execute() {
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec("rm -rf /");
    }
}
```

This is serialized, sent to port 9999, deserialized on the server (CYBER-001), and `execute()` is called — achieving direct, trivial RCE without needing gadget chains.

**Remediation:**

- Eliminate this pattern entirely. Do not invoke methods on deserialized objects from untrusted sources.
- If a command-dispatch pattern is needed, deserialize a **data-only** DTO and map it to a fixed set of server-side handlers:

```java
// Safe pattern: data-only message + server-side dispatch
record CommandRequest(String action, Map<String, String> params) {}

void dispatch(CommandRequest req) {
    switch (req.action()) {
        case "ping" -> handlePing(req.params());
        case "status" -> handleStatus(req.params());
        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown action");
    }
}
```

